A||||15||fiero56||transmission decision||||1||
Z||000000||fiero56||09-09-2000||10:56 PM||||After driving WT III's '85GT with barely 70K miles, I realize just how tired my '88 v-6 is. One difference is he has the stock 4-speed, and I have the stock 5-speed. I HATE my Getrag, the 3rd gear syncro sucks, 2nd is almost as bad. I have a 3.4 project that continually gets pushed back, and I am debating what tranny I should mate with my planned 3.4.<BR>I have never run my cars really hard(I might exercise my 3.4 a littler harder though), and am debating if I want a 4 or 5 speed. This will be my daily driver for at least a couple more years. I do a fair amount of highway travel, and can see much more of it coming in the nearer future. So highway gearing must be taken into account. I don't like shifting all the time, seems like I always am in town with the 5-speed. 4-speed has a really nice band for each gear.<P>At the moment, I am leaning towards a 4-speed, but would appreciate comments and opinions on what tranny to use.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 09-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Cooter||09-09-2000||11:22 PM||||I picked the 4 speed for my 88gt/3.4 because of my commute to work. It is pretty much all in town driving with a max speed of 55 mph (45 is the highest posted speed limit for the entire trip). I lucked up on an 84 tranny with the 4.10 gears and approx 70,000 miles on it. I love the performance and driveability of the combination. I have an Isuzu five speed as a back up, but have no intention of using it now.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||09-09-2000||11:52 PM||||If I remember correctly Cooter, you are using a 4.10(M19) tranny from an '84. If I were planning on only using the car for city use, that would be great, but I can see some pretty long distance travel at least several times a year coming up, and don't think I want to be running my engine that fast down the highway. Everyone I have talked with, says those M19 trannys hooked to 3.4s are sweet. <BR>I was thinking of the Muncie 4-speed, it came from the factory with the v-6/4-speed setup.||||reg||1
Z||000003||GT Bastard||09-10-2000||12:46 AM||||I heartily recommend 4sp's....  The 5sp's just don't seem to offer the same grunt.  0-60 lacks a bit.  4sp's are cheap too!  I'm running 4.10's and have learned to enjoy them and will appreciate them at the drag strip next summer.  However, 3.65 Muncie would be perfect in my opinion.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||TheFiero64||09-10-2000||11:29 AM||||I like my Isuzu 5 speed I have in my 88 V6. There are some folks that have been saying it shifts better than the getrag. I like the gearing, it seems great.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif"> ||||reg||3
Z||000005||Old Lar||09-10-2000||05:12 PM||||I like my 5 speed Getrag.  You can use third up to about 90 mph.  Remember forth and fith are overdrive gears.  Fith gear is great for the long cruise down the highway.<P>To be truthful I find any manual transmission car a pain when in traffic where an automatic transmission would be better.  For a daily driver in traffic get an automatic.  For fun driving get the Getrag repaired.<P>It sounds like you are unhappy with the Getrag because its broken. Get the syncros fixed.||||reg||10
Z||000006||fiero56||09-10-2000||05:55 PM||||I am a little unhappy with my Getrag. Even before the syncros were bad, it wasn't a very smooth shifting transmission. I think the Getrag is overrated, and to me, it isn't worth the cost of having it rebuilt(the cost to have a Getrag rebuilt is much more than it is worth to me). When the engine swap is completed I will probably just junk the transmission. Maybe I should drive a lower mileage unit and see how I like it before I rule it out. Thanks for the input, I might have to re-evaluate my consideration of the Getrag.<P>Muncie owners: how do you find around town gearing on your tranny? Not enough gears for varied speeds, or is it fine for around town use. How 'bout the highway? What type of RPMs are you turning?||||reg||1
Z||000007||Monkeyman||09-10-2000||09:47 PM||||This is an interesting question.  I currently have an automatic, but thought that I might swap out to a stick when I get my 3.4 (either next summer or the one after).  What's the advantage of the Getrag?  The Izusu 5spd?  Izusu 4spd?  Most of my driving is around town, but when I go out of town, it's usually at least a 156 mile trip (one way).||||reg||1
Z||000008||Shiner||09-10-2000||10:04 PM||||How much DOES it cost to have the synchros replaced on a getrag?||||reg||1
Z||000009||Cooter||09-10-2000||10:05 PM||||The guy who bought my 88 t-top car also has an 86 GT with the 4 speed. He says that at highway speeds, the difference between the getrag in the 88 and the muncie is hardly noticeable. Even with my tranny, I would not be concerned about taking a trip in the car. The way I see it, the 4.10's used to be behind a 4 banger and they held up okay, so a better v6 should live a pretty happy life. In the "for what it's worth department" my Fiero turns the same rpm's at 65 as my Civic does, and the Civic has 155,000 miles on it.||||reg||1
Z||000010||rockcrawl||09-10-2000||10:26 PM||||Approximate engine RPM in fourth gear with my 86 GT 3.8 with muncie 4spd and stock tire sizes:<P>40mph=1600rpm<BR>50mph=2000rpm<BR>60mph=2400rpm<BR>70mph=2700rpm<BR>80mph=3000rpm<BR>90mph=3500rpm<P>These numbers seem pretty good to me and the car drives well. I don't have any complaints. Don't even think about scrapping that old busted Getrag. There are plenty of people (like me) that would love to have it.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||1
Z||000011||fiero56||09-10-2000||10:28 PM||||I know the brass for the Getrag can be VERY expensive, $900 for parts alone for a complete rebuild of high wear items, then add labor charges to that. <BR>Take into account mine has over 160,000 miles(probably many more before I get the new engine in), and the input shaft bearing has a little too much wear on it, I don't have many plans for it. If the Muncie and the Getrag have very similar highway gearing, I might just go with the Muncie. They are plentiful, relatively cheap, if one breaks, just slap in another. Plus, if I don't like the Muncie, I can always put in a Getrag.<P>I still need to find a nice low mileage stock v-6 5-speed to drive for a few hours, anybody want to volunteer their car for the job <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Monkeyman||09-10-2000||10:37 PM||||What's the difference in cost between a Muncie and a Getrag (straight out purchase--rebuilt condition)??  What's the difference in the rebuild cost??||||reg||1
Z||000013||fiero56||09-11-2000||12:28 AM||||I haven't looked into buying a rebiult tranny. I have noticed that when you put the words "Fiero" and "Getrag" together, the price seems to magically double. If I go with a Muncie I will look for something lower mileage, higher if the price is right.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Standard||09-11-2000||04:24 AM||||I like my Getrag.. even though it's a bit notchy, and the third gear synchro is out.  I've been meaning to change the fluid and I think fresh syncromesh would get rid of some of the notchiness, and I just double-cluth it going into third gear..<P>Old Lar.. third up to 90mph?  I usually shift to fourth at 70...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  actually I thought the engine redlined at 80 or 85 in 3rd... guess I'll have to find an empty road and see for myself  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 150,000 miles and counting!       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">    <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker||||reg||1
Z||000015||fiero56||09-11-2000||11:10 PM||||I ran the Fiero down the highway tonight, at 70MPH it is turning exactly 2,500RPM. What engine RPM are you muncie owners getting at 70MPH? ||||reg||1
